    "data": {
      "A": "Research alternative valid grid-path algorithms for sparse obstacles on N up to 200,000: sort blocked coordinates and use binomial coefficients with inclusion-exclusion over earlier obstacles, avoiding O(N²) storage/work. Establish factorial/inverse-factorial or equivalent modular binomial computation within memory/time bounds. Preserve exact handling of blocked start/end, duplicate obstacle coordinates, and arbitrary valid inputs; compare against baseline on correctness and measured runtime.",
      "B": "Explore parallelization of the existing row-wise DP while reducing its dominant O(N²) costs. The row recurrence has a dependency across columns, so investigate a correct scan/prefix formulation or another proven equivalent; account for blocked-cell storage and ARM64 with only 4 CPU cores. Do not assume parallel overhead pays off: measure correctness and runtime against baseline, and retain the baseline if gains are absent.",
      "C": "Investigate a sparse-obstacle combinatorial method for counting monotone grid paths modulo 1,000,000,007, suitable for N<200,000 and M<0.01N² at large N. Derive and validate inclusion-exclusion using coordinates sorted by row/column and modular binomial coefficients; handle duplicate obstacles and endpoints. Benchmark on valid workloads and report correctness and performance rather than relying on theoretical gains alone.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated candidate implementations from A/B/C; preserve baseline as fallback/reference and the original input/output contract. Benchmark integrated candidates independently because combined changes may regress; select the fastest correct implementation based on measured results on the 4-core ARM64 target. Check edge cases including N=1, no obstacles, blocked endpoints, duplicate blocked cells, and obstacle ordering. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside the permitted integration scope.",
      "analysis": "This run is Problem D: count monotone paths in an N×N grid with blocked cells, modulo 1e9+7. Baseline is correct on four cases but takes about 0.0808 s and uses O(N²) blocked-cell storage and O(N²) DP, despite the stated sparse-obstacle regime for large N. The strongest distinct research direction is combinatorial counting via binomial coefficients and obstacle inclusion-exclusion, reducing dependence on N²; A and C can independently explore/validate variants. B researches a valid DP/scan improvement as a fallback direction. Four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU constrain parallel strategies; performance must be measured, not presumed."
